  "final_text": "Both objects exist in Kibana: the dashboard \"EFF-c2212ab5 Web logs overview\" and the alert rule \"EFF-c2212ab5 5xx spike\" (enabled, status `ok`).\n\n**Data explored:** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` view uses `timestamp` as its time field. The fields that matter here are `response` and `response.keyword` (status codes stored as strings, not numbers), `clientip`, `bytes`, `url.keyword`, and `extension`.\n\n**Dashboard (6 panels, time range last 1 year):**\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Average response size (average of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time by response code (bar, date histogram split by `response.keyword`)\n- Responses by status code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`)\n- Top requested URLs (table, terms on `url.keyword`)\n- 5xx responses (ES|QL metric: `WHERE response.keyword LIKE \"5*\"`)\n\n**Alert rule:**\n- Type `.es-query` (DSL), index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`\n- Filter: `response.keyword` wildcard `5*`\n- Runs every 1m, fires when count is greater than 10 over a 5m window\n- I tagged it `EFF-c2212ab5` and created it enabled. Disable it if you only wanted it defined.\n- No connector is attached, so it will evaluate but won't send notifications. `list_connectors` returned none.\n\n**Not verified:** I could not run direct queries against Elasticsearch from here. The Kibana ES|QL endpoints I tried returned 404 or 400. So I have not confirmed that the sample data contains 5xx codes, or that the rule's threshold would ever trip on this data. The dashboard panels will show that once they render.",
